A||N||12||jhogans||Need help in learning about automotive science?||||5||
Z||000000||jhogans||01-05-2000||11:40 PM||||OK guys.  I am a novice when it comes to engines, suspensions, etc, etc. etc.  When people talk about poly bushings, dog-bones, manifold, headers, etc. I'm lost.  Where can a guy get good information on learning about cars?  What books do you recommend that I read to learn about the intricate parts of an engine and how they work?  I figure this will put me in the right steps into diagnosing engine problems if I knew which engine part did what and what fluids flow through the parts.  <P>Thanks for any info.||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||01-05-2000||11:52 PM||||I recommend you get a Haynes repair manual. You can get them for ten bucks, and they tell a lot of stuff. Although, very informative for the novice, they don't offer a lot of help when it comes down to actually doing repairs. I'm sure others will recommend other books and such, but I started out with a Haynes, and have picked up the rest as I went along.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2FAST4U||01-06-2000||12:49 AM||||I mhave a book called "Automotive Engines" that I picked ip at a book sale years ago. I only covers engines and not the whole car but it explanes all of the different parts and systems of an engine, how they operate, and the principals and sciences behind the operation of a modern internal combustion engine. I you promise to return it, I'll let you borrow it. Just send me a mailing address.<BR>Also I read such magazines as Car Craft, Hot Rod, ond other magazines that you can find at any news Stand. Even though I've been involved in the automotive field in one form or another for over 30 years I still find leean things from the technical articles in such magazines. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||RZURCTN||01-06-2000||02:25 AM||||Run a search at your local library.  They are sure to have the kinds of books you're looking for.  And it won't cost you anything to learn.  Well, maybe some late charges  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||01-06-2000||01:52 PM||||The best books are used as texts in Auto shop programs at vocational schools.  Your best bet is to either head to the nearest joint-vocational or to a large bookseller like Barnes and Noble etc.  I never took Auto shop, but my college roomate did.  I read his auto shop book cover to cover because I found it so interesting.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000005||deceler8||01-06-2000||02:15 PM||||Check out your local community college to see if they offer any courses that might be of interest. Most of the time these courses allow you to work on an actual car, and sometimes even on your own car.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||01-06-2000||06:30 PM||||Ask your local tech school if they have any old text books for sale.  They may not be up to date, but you don't want to work on a brand new car either.  I bought a few books for next to nothing.||||reg||1
Z||000007||AL||01-07-2000||12:05 AM||||ok jhogan, try this book, it's called automotive encyclopedia, by godheart-wilcox. ths is one of the books my teacher told me to buy so i could get a headstart, even thou this was 17 yrs ago i know this book is still being made, it covers every part of the car from top to bottom or as we say bumper to bumper.good luck and start learning.||||reg||14
Z||000008||AL||01-07-2000||12:10 AM||||ok i don't now if this will appear twice but owell. ok hogan buy this book it's called automotive encyclopedia by goodheart-wilcox.this book covers from a to z , this was a book i was told to buy, at the time i was in school, 15 yrs ago but they still sell it so buy it and it will show you everything you want to know. ||||reg||14
